## Original Content
Moncloa asegura que la coordinación entre administraciones "nunca" ha sido un problema en el mes que está a punto de cumplirse de la entrada masiva de 80.000 inmigrantes irregulares en la ciudad.

## Summary
The Spanish Executive argues that the situation in Ceuta would remain the same today even if a unified command had been implemented after the massive entry of 80,000 irregular immigrants. Moncloa assures that coordination between administrations has not been an issue in the past month since the incident. The statement defends governmental handling of the immigration crisis.

## Key Points
- The Executive maintains that adopting a single command would not have changed the current situation in Ceuta post-immigration event.
- Moncloa claims that administrative coordination has been effective and problem-free during the past month.
- Approximately 80,000 irregular immigrants entered Ceuta in a massive event about a month ago.
- The article addresses concerns about governmental management and coordination in response to large-scale immigration.


